{
  "dom_id": "step-q94695",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The heights (in meters) of the trees in a particular park are normally distributed with a mean of $9\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{m}.$ Assuming that the heights of the trees in the park are normally distributed, what is the probability that a randomly selected tree is less than $10\\,\\text{m}$ tall? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\u03a6(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\u03a6(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the heights (in meters) of the trees in the park. Then we have $X\u223cN(9,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<10).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=P(X\\le 10)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{10-9}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.25) \\\\  & =\u03a6(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\u03a6(0.25)=0.5987\u22480.60.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of a randomly selected tree being shorter than $10\\,\\text{m}$ is approximately $0.60.$",
